1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,080
PayPal may retire its old copy-and-paste
buttons after January 1, 2027.

2
00:00:06,120 --> 00:00:07,760
This is Last Button.

3
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:11,466
Paste a site's address. It finds the old
buttons,

4
00:00:11,506 --> 00:00:14,569
AI rebuilds each one as a PayPal Payment
Link,

5
00:00:14,609 --> 00:00:18,050
and a test buyer proves every link takes a
payment.

6
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:23,855
Here's a community theatre site from 2010.
Its ticket,

7
00:00:23,895 --> 00:00:27,803
T-shirt and raffle buttons were pasted
from PayPal years ago,

8
00:00:27,843 --> 00:00:32,721
and two of them are saved or encrypted, so
the price isn't even in the code.

9
00:00:32,761 --> 00:00:36,450
The owner isn't a developer. They just
paste the address.

10
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:44,653
Nine buttons found, seven may stop
working.

11
00:00:44,693 --> 00:00:49,265
Readable buttons are copied field by
field. For the hidden season pass,

12
00:00:49,305 --> 00:00:53,551
Gemini reads the page, and it has to quote
the page word for word,

13
00:00:53,591 --> 00:00:57,903
or its answer is thrown away. Donate and
Subscribe aren't affected,

14
00:00:57,943 --> 00:01:02,230
so they're left alone. Anything the AI
priced waits for one click.

15
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:15,975
The AI only fills gaps, with structured
output, a schema check and a fallback model.

16
00:01:16,015 --> 00:01:18,300
It never sets a price on its own.

17
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:25,394
One click creates a PayPal Payment Link
for each product, with the same sizes,

18
00:01:25,434 --> 00:01:27,947
per-size prices, postage and tax.

19
00:01:27,987 --> 00:01:33,480
Here's the T-shirt on PayPal's own page:
XL at $21, plus $4.50 postage.

20
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:44,470
Under the hood: the Payment Links API,
Orders v2 to read every payment back,

21
00:01:44,510 --> 00:01:46,650
and a signed PayPal webhook.

22
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:53,678
Now the proof. A test buyer pays through
every new link in the PayPal sandbox,

23
00:01:53,718 --> 00:01:59,646
picking the dearest option. Then Last
Button asks PayPal what was actually paid,

24
00:01:59,686 --> 00:02:03,973
and checks it against price, postage and
tax, to the cent.

25
00:02:04,013 --> 00:02:09,716
Only then does a row turn green. The
poster bundle: $12, $3 postage, 99¢ tax.

26
00:02:09,756 --> 00:02:10,950
$15.99, exactly.

27
00:02:21,900 --> 00:02:27,133
To swap them in, download the patched
page, or paste one line into your template.

28
00:02:27,173 --> 00:02:32,250
There's product markup too, so AI shopping
agents can find a working checkout.

29
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:45,691
On our four test sites every field was
right, but we wrote those sites ourselves.

30
00:02:45,731 --> 00:02:47,780
Live, 7 of 7 payments matched.

31
00:02:49,700 --> 00:02:53,020
Last Button. Built with PayPal and Gemini.

